# BajaSplice

Sequence models for splicing, with the controls that decide whether the numbers
mean anything.

Four supervised tasks plus de novo cryptic exon detection. Every task ships with
a baseline a model has to beat before its score is worth quoting, because on
this kind of data most impressive-looking metrics come from exon length, GC
content, or class imbalance rather than from regulatory sequence.

## Results

All on chromosome-disjoint held-out data (test = chr1, 3, 5, 7, 9).

**Splice sites** — 397,770,000 test positions

| model | acceptor PR-AUC | donor PR-AUC |
|---|---|---|
| PWM baseline (motif only) | 0.066 | 0.103 |
| SpliceNet ctx-2000 | **0.935** | **0.931** |

**Preferred exons / PSI** — 196,967 test events, 54 GTEx tissues

| model | Pearson | preferred AUC | alt-subset r |
|---|---|---|---|
| geometry only (length/GC/frame) | 0.211 | 0.670 | 0.201 |
| splice-site PWM only | 0.192 | 0.684 | 0.155 |
| geometry + PWM | 0.304 | 0.749 | 0.253 |
| PSINet | **0.764** | **0.965** | **0.697** |

87.9% of annotated internal exons are constitutive, so the alt-subset column —
computed only on the 25,792 exons that are actually skipped somewhere — is the
number that matters. Labels validate against VastDB (independent samples and
quantification method) at **r = 0.935** on those exons.

**Competing splice sites** — 26,345 groups

| model | top-1 accuracy | usage r |
|---|---|---|
| random / distal / proximal | 0.450 / 0.425 / 0.471 | — |
| strongest PWM site | 0.512 | 0.226 |
| AltSSNet | **0.766** | **0.709** |

**Cryptic exons** — 691 TDP-43-induced sites discovered de novo from recount3

AUC **0.866** against 13,816 AG/GT dinucleotide-matched decoys from the same
introns. These sites were labelled *negative* in training, so the model was
taught to suppress them and still ranks them above matched decoys. Absolute
scores stay low: only 26% exceed 0.5, so **use rank, not probability**.

## Negative results, kept on purpose

**RBP knockdown response is not predictable from local sequence.** Mean per-RBP
AUC 0.573 for the sequence CNN vs 0.512 for geometry alone, against a
leave-one-out exon-responsiveness reference of 0.851. The CNN beat that
reference on 0 of 475 RBPs. Adding predicted eCLIP binding moved it to 0.577.

**Predicted RBP binding does not identify which exons respond to that RBP's
knockdown.** Matched RBP binding scores AUC 0.549; *mismatched* RBP binding
scores 0.549 too. Remove the shared per-exon component and the matched signal
is chance. This is not a defect of the binding predictor — on held-out eCLIP
data the same model distinguishes RBPs genuinely (0.698 vs 0.516 mismatched).
Binding simply does not predict regulation, and knockdown effects are largely
indirect.

## Bundled models

All five trained checkpoints ship inside the package (13 MB total), so every
model works from a bare install with no data root. They are small because the
architectures are small — dilated CNNs of 0.36–0.82M parameters.

| checkpoint | params | what it does |
|---|---|---|
| `ss_ctx2000` | 0.36M | splice sites, per-nucleotide |
| `psi_ctx2000` | 0.71M | cassette-exon PSI across 54 tissues |
| `altss` | 0.39M | which competing splice site wins |
| `rbp` / `rbp_bind` | 0.82M | RBP knockdown response |

`rbp` and `rbp_bind` are shipped to make a **negative** result reproducible
(per-RBP AUC 0.573 / 0.577 against a 0.851 reference), not because they predict
well.

### Bundled gene index

A slim gene index ships too (`bajasplice/data/genes.slim.sqlite`, 10 MB): one
canonical transcript per gene for all 77,081 genes, with exon coordinates packed
as an int32 blob rather than a row each. That is 1/32 the size of the full
index and enough for gene-name lookup, scanning, ranking and plotting.

So a bare install needs **only a genome FASTA**. Everything else is in the
package.

Index resolution: `<root>/data/processed/genes.sqlite` if it exists, else the
bundled slim index. Build the full one with `bajasplice prepare geneindex`.

**The two are not equivalent, and the difference is not cosmetic.** The full
index knows every annotated transcript; the slim one knows only the canonical
one. Since `rank_candidates` excludes annotated splice sites, they exclude
different sets:

| | full index | slim index |
|---|---|---|
| UNC13A donor candidates | 4,115 | 4,186 |
| known UNC13A cryptic donor | rank 44 | rank 50 |
| STMN2 cryptic acceptor | **excluded** | **rank 2** |

STMN2 is the instructive case. GENCODE v50 annotates that cryptic exon in a
minor isoform, so the full index treats it as a known site and drops it from
the candidate list entirely. The slim index only knows the canonical
transcript, so it surfaces — which for cryptic exon discovery is usually what
you want. `rank_candidates` records which index it used in
`df.attrs["index"]`, and the CLI prints it, so results are never silently
scoped.

A genome FASTA is required either way — the models score sequence, so sequence
has to come from somewhere.

Checkpoint resolution, in order:

1. explicit `--ckpt` / `ckpt=` argument
2. `BAJASPLICE_CKPT`
3. `<root>/models/ss_ctx2000.pt`
4. the bundled copy

A retrained model in the data root therefore wins over the bundled one, so
shipping weights never silently overrides your own work.

The PSI, competing-site and RBP checkpoints are **not** bundled. They live in
`<root>/models/` because they are only used by the evaluation pipeline, which
needs the datasets anyway.

## Install

```bash
pip install -e .
bajasplice config \
  --root ~/ml/splicing \
  --genome-fasta /path/GRCh38.primary_assembly.genome.fa \
  --gencode-gtf /path/gencode.v50.annotation.gtf \
  --save
```

Optional, only needed for the ENCODE and eCLIP steps:
`--gencode-v29-gtf` (matches ENCODE kallisto quantifications),
`--bajaclip-bundle`, `--eclip-dataset`.

Paths resolve in the order: explicit argument, environment variable
(`BAJASPLICE_ROOT`, `BAJASPLICE_GENOME`, ...), `~/.config/bajasplice/config.json`,
then defaults. Nothing in the package hardcodes a path.

## Use

```bash
bajasplice prepare gencode            # GTF -> flat exon table
bajasplice prepare gtex               # 17,382 samples -> per-tissue junction counts
bajasplice prepare events             # introns, cassette events + PSI, competing sites

bajasplice train splicesite --context 2000 --epochs 8
bajasplice train psi --context 2000 --init-from models/ss_ctx2000.pt
bajasplice train altss --init-from models/ss_ctx2000.pt

bajasplice evaluate baseline-splicesite
bajasplice evaluate baseline-psi
bajasplice evaluate vastdb
bajasplice evaluate report            # one comparison table for everything

bajasplice scan --gene TARDBP --top 10
bajasplice scan --gene UNC13A --rank acceptor --top 20
```

### Plotting across a transcript

Emits a JSON payload shaped for a client `TrackLayer` / `AttributionLayer`, or
a static PNG.

```bash
# whole-transcript acceptor and donor score tracks
bajasplice plot --gene TARDBP --out tardbp.json --png tardbp.png

# add per-base attribution for the two top predicted acceptors
bajasplice plot --gene TARDBP --top-sites 2 --which acceptor --window 60 --out t.json

# attribute one specific site, gradient instead of in-silico mutagenesis
bajasplice plot --gene UNC13A --site 17642413 --which donor --method gradient --out u.json
```

`--transcript` selects what the track draws: `canonical` (default, MANE Select
where annotated), `all` (union of every annotated exon, which for a
well-annotated gene is dozens of overlapping blocks), or a transcript id.

The payload:

```jsonc
{
  "schema": "bajasplice.track/1",
  "gene": "TARDBP",
  "track": {
    "name": "TARDBP", "chrom": "chr1", "strand": "+",
    "transcript_id": "ENST00000240185.8",
    "xmin": 11012344, "xmax": 11037991,
    "exons":   [{"xi": 11012654, "xf": 11012743}, ...],   // xi/xf: getExons()
    "introns": [{"xi": ..., "xf": ...}, ...]
  },
  "layers": [{
    "name": "TARDBP acceptor@11020262 (ism)",
    "type": "AttributionLayer",
    "attribution_type": "acceptor_attribution",  // or acceptor_score / donor_score
    "attribution_site": 11020262,                // null for whole-gene score tracks
    "window": 60,                                // null for score tracks
    "xmin": ..., "xmax": ..., "ymin": ..., "ymax": ...,
    "site_score": 0.88839,
    "points": {"A": [{"x": ..., "y": ...}], "T": [...], "C": [...], "G": [...]}
  }]
}
```

Points are already split by the **reference base** at each position, matching
what the client's `addAttributionPoint(x, y, base)` does, so they load straight
into `apts` / `tpts` / `cpts` / `gpts`:

```js
const p = await (await fetch('tardbp.json')).json();
for (const L of p.layers) {
  const layer = new AttributionLayer(
      L.name, L.xmin, L.ymin, L.xmax, L.ymax,
      L.attribution_type, L.attribution_site, L.window, track);
  layer.apts = L.points.A;   // already {x, y}
  layer.tpts = L.points.T;
  layer.cpts = L.points.C;
  layer.gpts = L.points.G;
  track.addLayer(layer);
}
```

Two things worth knowing. `attribution_type` containing `acceptor_attribution`
switches the client to its purple/blue palette, so score tracks are named
`*_score` to stay on the other one. And base colouring is only informative on
attribution layers: on a score track nearly every point is a `G`, because an
acceptor is the G of `AG` and a donor the G of `GT`.

`--min-score` (default 1e-3) drops the noise floor from score tracks. A 25 kb
gene goes from ~25,000 points to ~50 without losing anything visible.

### Attribution

`ism` (default) mutates every position in the window to each of the three
alternatives and measures how far the site's score falls, so a positive value
means the reference base holds the site up. It costs `3 x 2 x window` forward
passes. `gradient` is one backward pass and scales to whole genes, but is a
local linearisation rather than a real perturbation.

On a strong TARDBP acceptor the top ISM attributions land on the `AG`
dinucleotide itself, which is the sanity check to run on a new model.

```python
import bajasplice
from bajasplice.scan import scan_gene, rank_candidates
from bajasplice.tracks import build_payload, render_png

acc, don, (chrom, start, end, strand) = scan_gene("STMN2")
cands = rank_candidates("UNC13A", "acceptor")   # annotated sites excluded

payload = build_payload("STMN2", sites=[79616821], which="acceptor", window=80)
render_png(payload, "stmn2.png")
```

## Coordinate conventions

Getting these wrong is silent, so they are pinned in `tests/test_coordinates.py`.

- Introns are 1-based inclusive: `exon_end + 1 .. next_exon_start - 1`.
- GTEx junction ids are `chr_intronStart_intronEnd` in those coordinates.
- A donor is the **first** intronic base, an acceptor the **last**, both in
  transcript orientation.
- `GenomeReader.codes(..., strand="-")` returns reverse-complemented sequence,
  so callers pick coordinates and never handle strand themselves.

## Layout

```
bajasplice/
  config.py        paths and chromosome splits; the only place paths live
  genome.py        FASTA access, one-hot encoding
  models.py        SpliceNet, PSINet, AltSSNet, RBPBindingNet
  datasets.py      the four torch Datasets
  bajaclip.py      adapter for an external eCLIP binding predictor
  scan.py          gene/interval scanning and cryptic candidate ranking
  attribution.py   in-silico mutagenesis and gradient x input
  tracks.py        client plot payloads and static PNG rendering
  prepare/         gencode, gtex, events, encode, binding, cryptic
  train/           splicesite, psi, altss, rbp
  evaluate/        baselines, metrics, vastdb, eclip, cryptic, report
```

## Pitfalls this package encodes

Each of these cost real debugging time and is now either tested or documented
in the module that hit it.

- **Minus-strand chunked scanning.** Reversing a scan track once at the end
  scrambles chunk order. Reverse within each chunk (`scan.chunk_offsets`,
  pinned in `tests/test_scan.py`).
- **Leaky responsiveness control.** Exon marginal responsiveness must be
  leave-one-RBP-out or it contains the label it is a control for.
- **Cross-batch nulls.** ENCODE reuses one control across ~8.6 knockdowns, so
  random control-vs-control pairs measure batch, not noise, and produce a null
  *larger* than the real effect.
- **Ranking cryptic candidates.** Annotated splice sites of alternative
  transcripts sit outside exon bodies, survive an exon mask, score ~1.0, and
  fill the whole top of the ranking. Exclude them explicitly.
- **Constitutive imbalance.** 88% of internal exons never skip; report the
  alternative subset separately.
- **Positive controls.** Of three TDP-43 knockdown studies, one showed no STMN2
  induction. Check a known event fires before trusting a dataset.
